/// What a plural leaf means once a bare one no longer guesses.
///
/// `Widget` is declared by both `Alpha` and `Omega`. A module that imports
/// NEITHER names no type by writing it bare, and that half is now an error -
/// it lives in tests/tests/projects/plural_leaf_gate as a `compile_fail`
/// asserting E0155. What stays here is the other half: a module that DOES
/// import a declarer still binds the one it imported, which §4's rib chain
/// requires and which the rejection must not have broken.
///
/// These two are the reason the rejection can be told apart from a blunter
/// change. A fix that made every bare plural leaf unresolvable everywhere -
/// rather than only where nothing binds it - passes the gate project and fails
/// HERE. That is the whole point of keeping them.
///
/// ---- WHAT THIS USED TO PIN, AND WHY THE HOST MATTERED -----------------
///
/// Before the rejection, the bare leaf in an importing-nothing module resolved
/// anyway, through the arena's global leaf index. `register_leaf_name` is
/// first-registration-wins and compares no names, and registration order is
/// depth-first module discovery seeded by the order the FILESYSTEM enumerates
/// the directory - so which declarer won was invisible to any reader of the
/// source, and copying a directory could change what the program compiled to.
/// Measured with controls in docs/name-resolution.md §8.2y.
///
/// That is also why the import control below reads `Omega` rather than either:
/// where the index picked `Alpha`, binding `Omega` here could not be luck.
/// The index no longer answers, so the expectation is now simply what the
/// import says, and it no longer depends on the host.
///
![config(testing)]
namespace ResolutionLeafIndex::LeafIndexTest;

import std::core::result;
import std::test::error;
import std::test::error::{ TestError };
import std::test::assert;
import std::test::assert::{ expect_eq };

/// CONTROL. The leaf really is declared twice, by two distinct types.
/// Without it the import control below, and the E0155 gate in
/// plural_leaf_gate, would both be satisfied by a program in which
/// `Omega::Widget` did not exist or had collapsed into `Alpha`'s - and each
/// would then be exercising the UNIQUE-leaf case, which is E0240 and a
/// different rule.
![test]
function CONTROL_the_leaf_is_declared_by_two_distinct_types()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    mut a: ResolutionLeafIndex::Alpha::Widget<i64> =
        ResolutionLeafIndex::Alpha::Widget::<i64> { v: 17 };
    mut o: ResolutionLeafIndex::Omega::Widget<i64> =
        ResolutionLeafIndex::Omega::Widget::<i64> { v: 19 };
    expect_eq(a.tag(), 1)?;
    expect_eq(o.tag(), 2)?;
    expect_eq(a.unwrap(), 17 as i64)?;
    expect_eq(o.unwrap(), 19 as i64)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}

/// CONTROL, and the one that says what the defect actually is. The SAME plural
/// leaf, named from a module that imports one declarer, binds THAT declarer.
/// Where the leaf index picks `Alpha`, `Omega` here cannot be the arbitrary
/// answer landing correctly by luck; where it picks `Omega`, the two coincide
/// and this stops discriminating -- see the note in `importer.cryo` and
/// docs/name-resolution.md §8.2y.
///
/// It is also the guard on the fix: a change that made `Orphan` an error by
/// making a bare plural leaf unresolvable everywhere would break this too, and
/// that is not the specified behavior -- §4's rib chain says an imported
/// module's export set is in scope.
![test]
function CONTROL_an_importing_module_binds_the_module_it_imported()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    mut w: ResolutionLeafIndex::Omega::Widget<i64> =
        ResolutionLeafIndex::Importer::Packer::pack(23);
    expect_eq(w.unwrap(), 23 as i64)?;
    expect_eq(w.tag(), 2)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}
